---
/**
 * Track 9 (Statistics & Probability for AI), Lesson 11, illustration 2 of 2.
 *
 * Standard error as a function of sample size. SE = σ / √n. With σ = 4
 * we plot SE from n = 1 to n = 200, watching it fall along a 1/√n curve.
 * Annotated checkpoints: doubling n cuts SE by a factor of √2 ≈ 1.41;
 * quadrupling n halves SE. A few specific n values from L11a (5, 30, 100)
 * are marked to anchor the two pictures together.
 */

const cx = 100;
const cy = 280;
const PLOT_W = 540;
const PLOT_H = 200;

const SIGMA = 4;
const N_MIN = 1;
const N_MAX = 200;
const SE_MAX = SIGMA / Math.sqrt(N_MIN); // 4
const SAMPLES = 200;

const sx = (n: number) => cx + ((n - N_MIN) / (N_MAX - N_MIN)) * PLOT_W;
const sy = (se: number) => cy - (se / SE_MAX) * PLOT_H;

const SE = (n: number) => SIGMA / Math.sqrt(n);

let curvePath = '';
for (let i = 0; i <= SAMPLES; i++) {
	const n = N_MIN + ((N_MAX - N_MIN) * i) / SAMPLES;
	curvePath += `${i === 0 ? 'M' : 'L'} ${sx(n).toFixed(1)} ${sy(SE(n)).toFixed(1)} `;
}

const checkpoints = [5, 30, 100];

const ACCENT = '#7c3aed';
const BLUE = '#3b82f6';
const AMBER = '#d97706';

void BLUE;
---

<figure class="cw-illus">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 740 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="sp-sesh-title sp-sesh-desc">
		<title id="sp-sesh-title">Standard error as a function of sample size: SE equals sigma over square root n, shrinking along a one-over-square-root curve</title>
		<desc id="sp-sesh-desc">A coordinate plot with sample size n on the horizontal axis from 1 to 200 and standard error on the vertical axis from 0 to about 4. An accent purple curve traces SE equals 4 over square root of n, falling steeply at first then leveling off. Three amber circles at n equals 5, 30, 100 mark the same checkpoints used in the L11 sampling-distribution picture, with values 1.79, 0.73, and 0.40 labeled. A legend on the right notes that doubling n cuts SE by about 1.41, and quadrupling n halves it.</desc>

		{/* axes */}
		<line x1={cx} y1={cy} x2={cx + PLOT_W + 10} y2={cy} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.1" opacity="0.55" />
		<line x1={cx} y1={cy} x2={cx} y2={cy - PLOT_H - 10} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.1" opacity="0.55" />
		<text x={cx + PLOT_W + 14} y={cy + 4} font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.65">n</text>
		<text x={cx - 6} y={cy - PLOT_H - 12} text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.65">SE</text>

		{/* x ticks */}
		{[1, 30, 60, 100, 150, 200].map((t) => (
			<g>
				<line x1={sx(t)} y1={cy} x2={sx(t)} y2={cy + 5} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0.7" opacity="0.5" />
				<text x={sx(t)} y={cy + 18} text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">{t}</text>
			</g>
		))}

		{/* y ticks */}
		{[1, 2, 3, 4].map((t) => (
			<g>
				<line x1={cx - 5} y1={sy(t)} x2={cx} y2={sy(t)} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0.7" opacity="0.5" />
				<text x={cx - 8} y={sy(t) + 4} text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">{t}</text>
			</g>
		))}

		{/* curve */}
		<path d={curvePath.trim()} fill="none" stroke={ACCENT} stroke-width="2.4" />

		{/* checkpoint markers */}
		{checkpoints.map((n) => (
			<g>
				<line x1={sx(n)} y1={cy} x2={sx(n)} y2={sy(SE(n))} stroke={AMBER} stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" opacity="0.6" />
				<line x1={cx} y1={sy(SE(n))} x2={sx(n)} y2={sy(SE(n))} stroke={AMBER} stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" opacity="0.6" />
				<circle cx={sx(n)} cy={sy(SE(n))} r="4.4" fill={AMBER} />
				<text x={sx(n) + 8} y={sy(SE(n)) - 6} font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill={AMBER}>n={n}, SE={SE(n).toFixed(2)}</text>
			</g>
		))}

		{/* legend */}
		<g transform="translate(440, 60)">
			<rect x="0" y="0" width="280" height="120" rx="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.04" />
			<rect x="0" y="0" width="280" height="120" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.3" />
			<text x="140" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.7">standard error rule:</text>
			<text x="140" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill={ACCENT}>
				SE = σ / √n
			</text>
			<text x="140" y="76" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.8">
				double n → SE × 1/√2 ≈ 0.71
			</text>
			<text x="140" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.8">
				quadruple n → SE × 1/2 = 0.50
			</text>
		</g>
	</svg>
	<figcaption>Standard error falls as one over the square root of n, so the curve drops steeply at first and then levels off. To halve your uncertainty, you need to quadruple your sample. The three amber checkpoints (n = 5, 30, 100) match the three bells in the companion CLT picture; that is where 1.79, 0.73, and 0.40 come from.</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
	.cw-illus { margin: 1.75rem auto; max-width: 100%; }
	.cw-illus svg { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 740px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto; }
	.cw-illus figcaption { text-align: center; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic; }
</style>
